<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n36573" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">planto</span></b> , āre, v. a.
		planta. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>To set</i>, <i>plant</i>, <i>transplant</i> (cf. sero): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">hoc modo plantantur punicae,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Plin.
					17, 10, 13, § 67</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Pall. 4, 5</span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">vineam,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vulg.
					Luc. 20, 9</span></i>
			 et saep. —</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>To fix in place</i>, and hence, <i>to
				form</i>, <i>make</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">qui plantavit aurem, non audiet?</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vulg.
					Psa. 93, 9</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
